<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Hum Honge Kaamyab (Girija Kumar Mathur)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Hindi translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Amra Korbo Joy (Hemanga Biswas; recorded by Bhupen Hazarika)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Bengali translation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ek Din Shurjer Bhor (Shibdas Bandyopadhyay; Calcutta Youth Choir)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1971</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Bengali version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nammal Vijayikkum (N. P. Chandrasekharan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">late 1970s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Malayalam translation, same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Venceremos (María Elena Walsh)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Spanish version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Free the People (Northern Ireland civil rights movement)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">its title sung at the end of each verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony by William Rowland</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, crediting Tindley</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Where Have All the Flowers Gone: The Songs of Pete Seeger (Bruce Springsteen)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1998</span><span class="sg-chain-h">reinterpretation; also on We Shall Overcome: The Seeger Sessions, 2006</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Roger Waters</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new version, a protest against the blockade of Gaza</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">My Name Is Khan (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2010</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in English and Hindi</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Orion test flight (arranged by Nolan Williams Jr., sung by Denyce Graves)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2014</span><span class="sg-chain-h">recording sent to space</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dropkick Murphys</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2022</span><span class="sg-chain-h">single and music video</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">I'll Overcome Some Day (Charles Albert Tindley)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1901</span><span class="sg-chain-h">thought to have shaped its structure; text and melody altered</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">No More Auction Block for Me (spiritual)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">printed 1867</span><span class="sg-chain-h">tune echoes its opening and closing melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">O sanctissima (Sicilian Mariners Hymn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1792</span><span class="sg-chain-h">resembles the first half</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">I'll Be All Right (hymn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">19th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">essentially the music of the second half</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">We'll Overcome (I'll Be All Right), as sung by Lucille Simmons</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1945–1946</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the version Zilphia Horton learned</span></li></ul></section>
